In this lesson, we'll look at a clever way to trap the solution of an equation inside a shrinking interval. Here's the idea. Suppose you have a continuous function, and you've found two input values, one on the left and one on the right, where the function's outputs have opposite signs, one positive and one negative. By the intermediate value theorem, the graph has to cross zero somewhere between them, so there's a root hiding in that interval. To find it, we keep cutting the interval in half. Start by setting your left and right bounds, then take their midpoint. Check the sign of the function at that midpoint. If it matches the sign on the left, the root must be to the right of the midpoint, so you move the left bound in. Otherwise, you move the right bound in. Repeat this halving until the interval is tiny, and the midpoint is your root to whatever precision you need. The beauty is that the interval shrinks exponentially, so even if you start with a huge gap, twenty or so steps give you accuracy to a millionth. Now let's see it in action.